<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Rueda de Jalón (Hudid)</h1><p class="st-meta">1110 CE – 1131 CE (approx.) · capital Rueda de Jalón</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last refuge of the Hudid kings of Zaragoza, a castle state on the Jalón kept alive through alliance with the Christian kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1110, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Hudid</a> heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-imad-al-dawla">Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla</a> withdrew to the strong castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rueda-de-jalon">Rueda de Jalón</a>, west of the city, and continued to rule a small territory from there, relying on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> against the Almoravids.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hud">Hudid</a> remnant was a client principality, surviving between Almoravid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and the advancing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon</a>. After Abd al-Malik's death in 1130 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafadola">Ahmad Sayf al-Dawla</a>, known to Christians as Zafadola, negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafadola">Zafadola</a> handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rueda-de-jalon">Rueda</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> in exchange for lands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> and became a Castilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>. He later led Andalusi revolts against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> and was killed in 1146. The castle passed to Aragon.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1110 CE – 1131 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.79%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1110 CE – 1131 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rueda de Jalón</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla, Ahmad Sayf al-Dawla (Zafadola)</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hudid Rueda</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1120&amp;focus=rueda-de-jalon-hudid" aria-label="Rueda de Jalón (Hudid) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4649 694 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Rueda de Jalón (Hudid) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4969 950L4979 949M4969 950L4910 928"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4979 949h0M4910 928h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4969 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4969 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rueda de Jalón. <a href="/atlas?year=1120&amp;focus=rueda-de-jalon-hudid">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
